A1. Be careful—do not break this. You might mess up a suit. ~ PROMISE
A8. One of the trials of an aviator. ~ ATTEMPT
A15. This is what a man does when he lies. ~ REPOSES
A16. This describes China. ~ FRAGILE
A17. This is wicked. ~ EVIL
A18. A goat without any go. ~ AT
A19. These are thrilling. ~ TINGLES
A20. The kind of people who never read the newspapers. (They are in a grave situation.) ~ DEAD
A21. This fell for the pied piper. ~ RAT
A23. Just for example this is an abbreviation. ~ EG
A24. What an Eskimo will do when he gets fat. ~ EAT
A25. You wiil not have much interest in this. ~ INT
A26. Stepped on it. ~ TROD
A28. The laboring classes never live in this. ~ EASE
A29. You will always see these hanging around a Chinese joint. ~ CUES
A31. A good thing to use while working the "Judge" puzzles. ~ HEAD
A32. This is often caused by back-seat driving. ~ ANGER
A33. You will be going some if you can pass this. ~ TEST
A34. This is not a bad word. ~ GOOD
A36. A feminine suffix. ~ ESS
A37. A day of the week. ~ YESTERDAY
A41. A boy's nickname. ~ SID
A44. A good thing to do to dictated letters. ~ READ
A45. Sun-tan. ~ ECRU
A49. This is known all over the country. ~ ONION
A51. Part of the family skeleton. ~ RIBS
A53. A singer that is often heard on the air. ~ WREN
A54. A matrimonial state. ~ UTAH
A55. To touch. ~ FEEL
A56. You will find this in the last chapter. ~ END
A57. This will stand for a relative but not an in-law. ~ REL
A58. You will find many different types here. ~ PI
A59. To dunk. ~ SOP
A61. A Persian tent manufacturer. ~ OMAR
A62. A town in Italy noted for its violins. (Don't confuse this with a town famous for vile inns.) ~ CREMONA
A65. You could make a high score with this trump. ~ NO
A66. This is what Nature is entitled. ~ DAME
A67. What Grandmother used to be. ~ ENCASED
A68. Indicates. ~ DENOTES
A70. These are always hunting dogs. ~ SETTERS
A71. Nero's wife. ~ EMPRESS

D1. This is what the weather man will do before the hot weather comes. ~ PREDICT
D2. This is what stamps are for. ~ REVENUE
D3. You will be thoughtless if you take these. ~ OPIATES
D4. This is a matter of form. ~ MOLD
D5. Always exists in puzzles. ~ IS
D6. To scorch. ~ SEAR
D7. This is what lots and lots and lots make. ~ ESTATE
D8. You will often find this toward the end of the deck. ~ AFT
D9. This is what the baby did before it walked. ~ TRIED
D10. Smack. ~ TANG
D11. This is one in a dozen. ~ EGG
D12. This is what comes out of gasoline. ~ MILEAGE
D13. This is what a Camel does. ~ PLEASES
D14. These have soft jobs in a mattress factory. ~ TESTERS
D22. These are always making a killing. ~ TRAGEDIES
D27. You are on the right scent now. ~ ODOR
D28. A short letter the author received from the printer. ~ EN
D30. A country home. ~ STY
D31. These are thin but have very shapely legs. ~ HOSE
D32. This might be "Sweet Adeline." ~ ADA
D35. When these are against you, you haven't much chance. ~ ODDS
D38. This old bird is always at sea. ~ ERN
D39. A weed. ~ TARE
D40. A European coniferous tree. (Now doesn't that sound just like you?) ~ YEW
D41. Starting points. ~ SOURCES
D42. This man is in the hospital for an operation. ~ INTERNE
D43. A speech made by a Scotchman. ~ DIALECT
D46. The last thing on earth a person wants to do. ~ CREMATE
D47. Designates again. ~ RENAMES
D48. This is what millionaires always do before they retire. ~ UNDRESS
D50. This is quite surprising. ~ OH
D52. Preferred locks. ~ BLONDE
D55. This is better than a fine. ~ FINER
D58. To sit pretty. ~ POSE
D60. A good come-back from the editor. ~ POEM
D61. This smells like Ver. No. 27. ~ ODOR
D63. A shoe cleaner. ~ MAT
D64. The best sellers. ~ ADS
D69. Namby Pamby (abbr.) ~ NP


